# Line endings matter here, not as tidiness but as correctness.
#
# setup.bat and setup.ps1 are run on Windows and must arrive with CRLF: a
# batch file checked out with bare LF endings can misbehave. setup.sh runs on
# Linux and must arrive with LF, or bash fails with an obscure "\r: command
# not found". Without these rules, a clone on the other platform breaks the
# installer, and the failure looks like a bug in the tool.

* text=auto eol=lf

*.bat text eol=crlf
*.ps1 text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf

*.sh  text eol=lf

# Data and binary formats: never touch.
*.xpt      binary
*.sas7bdat binary
*.parquet  binary
*.db       binary
*.key      binary
*.blob     binary
*.png      binary
*.zip      binary
*.tar.gz   binary

# Keep CSV diffs readable in review -- steward review sheets are read in PRs.
*.csv text eol=lf
